id: pm-beads-vulo
title: A truncated list-all answer is consumed as if it were complete
description: "Every list-all read in this package takes .items from the pm list-all --json envelope without checking the envelope's completeness receipt. Verified on this host against the 682-item workspace: pm 2026.8.14 returned 10 items with truncated=true, and the exporter happily emitted a 10-item export reporting success (98% of rows missing). 2026.8.15 fixes the default, but truncated can still go true under an output budget and completeness.status can go partial when items are unreadable — each of those must refuse loudly instead of producing a partial result that reports success. Related: the @unbrained/pm-cli dependency resolves through a floating range (>=2026.8.3), which is how the 2026.8.14 regression reached this package with no diff to review."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-15T10:02:06.253Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-15T16:08:58.498Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-15T10:34:57.295Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-15T10:34:57.295Z"
author: pi-agent
comments[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-15T10:25:04.171Z",pi-agent,"Wired all four refusal signals into the single list-all call site (readPmItems, which feeds buildBeadsFromWorkspace / runExport / readWorkspaceBeadIds fallback): truncated===true, has_more===true, completeness.status!==complete (missing receipt also trips), omission_receipt.has_omissions===true. Each refusal is a CommandError naming the tripped signal and the count-vs-total figures; no partial list, no warning-and-continue. maxBuffer kept (64 MiB, PM_JSON_MAX_BUFFER override) and the ENOBUFS null-status case is classified explicitly. Chose REFUSAL over a paging loop: this package has no legitimate use for a partial page.\n\nRevert-proof (removed only the assertListAllComplete(parsed) call, rebuilt, ran the new tests): all four refusal tests failed with 'AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: Missing expected exception' (truncated / has_more / completeness.status / omission_receipt at test/smoke.test.ts:1610 et al.); happy-path test still passed as expected. Fix restored, full suite green: 110/110.\n\nTests drive the refusal from a REAL pm list-all envelope captured from the real CLI (temp 3-item workspace, exact production argv), mutated one field each, injected through the new PmListAllSpawn seam defaulting to the real spawn. Exact-pin: devDependencies and peerDependencies both 2026.8.15 (no >=/^/~); node -p resolves 2026.8.15 after npm ci. Coverage 93.58 lines / 80.12 branches / 95.24 functions — thresholds unchanged."
  "2026-08-15T10:34:56.347Z",pi-agent,"PR #69 open and green (test 22/26, semgrep, DeepScan, Hound; Greptile pass after adopting its two review findings: non-array items payload now refuses, packaging test enforces dev/peer pin equality). Pushed commits: 8bd9635, 6a60526."
  "2026-08-15T14:03:36.970Z",claude-agent,"Review round 2 (CodeRabbit + Greptile on PR #69) — three findings adopted, one declined.\n\nADOPTED — `omission_receipt` mutation assumed the receipt exists (test/smoke.test.ts). The baseline test accepts `omission === undefined`, so on a CLI that omits the receipt the mutation assigned through `undefined` and threw a TypeError *outside* `assert.throws`: the test would have failed with the wrong error instead of proving the refusal. The mutation now creates the receipt when absent.\n\nADOPTED — `readPmItems surfaces child failures through the seam` had no `{ skip: !hasPmCli() }` guard while calling `realEnvelope()`. On a host without the pm CLI it failed its `init.status === 0` assertion rather than skipping. All four cases inject a seam and never reach a real child process, so the test now uses a temp directory and needs no CLI at all — strictly more coverage than adding a skip.\n\nADOPTED (Greptile P1, root cause was larger than reported) — the lockfile still carried the pre-floor exact peer `2026.8.15` while package.json declares `>=2026.8.15`, which would fail the release workflow's `npm ci` synchronization check. Regenerating the lockfile fixed that, but the audit also found `manifest.json` still advertising `pm_min_version: 2026.7.28` — the pm host's own load-time floor — while the code refuses on a completeness receipt that only exists from 2026.8.15. A 2026.7.28 host would have loaded an extension that cannot work against it. Floor corrected to 2026.8.15.\n\nDECLINED — CodeRabbit asked the packaging test to assert `declared === peer` exact equality. That was written against the earlier exact-peer revision; commit 1852bf1 deliberately moved the peer to a `>=` floor because an exact peer pin makes every later CLI patch a peer conflict for consumers under strict peer resolution, turning a compatibility statement into a lockstep release dependency. The test correctly asserts floor *shape* plus a `>= 2026.8.15` bound.\n\nDURABLE FIX — nothing bound package.json's peer floor to manifest.json's `pm_min_version`, which is why the manifest could sit three releases stale while every file stayed internally consistent. A regression test now binds them; reverting the floor to 2026.7.28 makes it fail (verified).\n"
  "2026-08-15T15:12:49.592Z",claude-agent,"Review round 3 (Greptile + CodeRabbit on PR #69) — three findings adopted, one declined.\n\nADOPTED (Greptile P1, and it was right about a real hole) — `readPmItems` refuses an incomplete or unusable `list-all` envelope, but `readWorkspaceBeadIds` caught that refusal and returned `undefined`, and `readAndValidateBeads` had a second `catch` doing the same. The refusal was therefore defeated one level up.\n\nThe consequence is a MISDIAGNOSIS, which is worse than a missing check: with `workspaceBeadIds === undefined`, `validateBeadsText` reports a dependency that exists in the workspace as a hard `dangling_dependency` error. The import gate then rejects a valid file and blames the operator's input for a workspace read that failed silently. Reproduced by reverting the fix, which yields exactly:\n\n```\nline 1 [dangling_dependency]: dependency references unknown bead id \"bd-missing\"\n```\n\nFixed by distinguishing the two cases rather than by removing the catch. \"No workspace here\" is legitimate and the cross-check is optional, so an ordinary read failure still degrades to \"no workspace data\". A completeness refusal is not that — the workspace answered and the answer is provably partial — so those now throw `IncompleteWorkspaceReadError` (a `CommandError` subclass) and both catch sites re-throw it. Regression test drives a fake `pm` emitting a truncated envelope through the real import gate and asserts the refusal arrives with `truncated=true` and WITHOUT the false `dangling_dependency` text.\n\nADOPTED (CodeRabbit) — the list-all seam discarded its argv, so nothing asserted what the production read actually sends. `--full --include-body` is what keeps descriptions, tags and dependency edges out of the brief projection, and the ABSENCE of `--limit` is what makes the read the whole workspace. Both were invisible to every existing test, because the receipt-based refusals all inject their own envelope and never exercise the argv. The seam now captures it and the test asserts the complete argv plus the explicit no-`--limit` requirement. Verified non-vacuous: adding `--limit 1000` to the production call fails it.\n\nADOPTED (CodeRabbit, trivial) — the new floor test repeated the `/^>=\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+$/` literal that `MINIMUM_VERSION_RANGE` already defines with its rationale. Now reuses the constant.\n\nDECLINED (CodeRabbit) — reword the CHANGELOG entry to state the fix rather than the defect. The entry is not hand-written: `pm-changelog` generates it from this item's title, and the line sits under `### Fixed`, where naming the bug is the Keep a Changelog convention and what all 20 sibling packages do. Rewording here would require retitling this Issue to describe a fix rather than the defect it was filed for — degrading the tracker to improve the changelog. The same finding was raised on pm-starter #58 and declined for the same reason. The constructive fix is a dedicated release-note field in pm-changelog, tracked separately.\n\n112 tests + the new cases pass, release:check green.\n"
  "2026-08-15T16:08:58.497Z",claude-agent,"Review round 4 (Greptile P1 on PR #69) — adopted, and it exposed an SDK-level defect underneath.\n\nFINDING — round 3 distinguished a completeness refusal (`IncompleteWorkspaceReadError`) from other read failures and let only the former propagate. Greptile correctly pointed out that the rest of the class was still swallowed: an ENOBUFS overrun on a large workspace, a nonzero exit, and malformed JSON all throw a plain `CommandError`, were all caught, and all became `undefined` — producing the same false `dangling_dependency` diagnosis the round-3 fix was meant to end.\n\nThe round-3 reasoning was right but the line was drawn in the wrong place. \"No workspace here\" versus \"a workspace we failed to read\" is the real distinction; \"incomplete\" versus \"everything else\" is not. Absence is now decided by testing for the tracker root BEFORE reading, rather than by inferring it from a failure afterwards, and the CLI read that follows is no longer wrapped in a catch at all. The second catch in `readAndValidateBeads` is gone for the same reason.\n\nWHAT THIS UNCOVERED — verifying the fix showed the CLI fallback is very nearly unreachable, because `listAllItemMetadata` does not fail the way the code assumed:\n\n    exists + readable, no items   -> [] (correct)\n    exists + chmod 000            -> [] (WRONG: unreadable reported as empty)\n    missing                       -> throws \"Tracker root does not exist\"\n\nSo the SDK reports an unreadable workspace as an empty one, and the `catch { fall back to pm list-all }` only fires for a missing root. The live exposure in this package is therefore upstream of the catch Greptile flagged: the empty id set can come from the SDK itself, and validation then reports an existing dependency as dangling. Filed as unbraind/pm-cli#1034, with the pm-beads consequence and the observation that the CLI already has the receipt machinery (`completeness.unreadable_item_count`) that this SDK surface omits.\n\nTESTS — the round-3 test drove a truncated envelope through a fake `pm` on PATH, which the SDK short-circuit made unreachable; keeping it would have been a test that passes for the wrong reason. Replaced with two that assert what is actually reachable: a missing workspace degrades rather than failing the gate, and a completeness refusal is a distinguishable subtype that is still a `CommandError` for existing handlers.\n\n115/115 tests, release:check green.\n"
files[4]{path,scope}:
  manifest.json,project
  package-lock.json,project
  test/packaging.test.ts,project
  test/smoke.test.ts,project
tests[1]:
  - command: npx tsx --test test/packaging.test.ts
    scope: project
    timeout_seconds: 300
    assert_stdout_contains[1]: the manifest host floor matches the package peer floor
    assert_stdout_regex[1]: fail 0$
    note: "Binds package.json peer floor to manifest.json pm_min_version. Non-vacuous: reverting the floor to 2026.7.28 fails this file (verified 2026-08-15)."
close_reason: "PR #69 (refuse-incomplete-list-all-and-exact-pin-cli) is open and green: list-all reads now refuse truncated/has_more/partial-completeness/omitted-fields envelopes with signal+counts in the message, CLI exact-pinned to 2026.8.15 in both dependency blocks, 111/111 tests including revert-proven refusal tests."
body: ""
